//! crypto/kumara_subkey.rye — Season G (Cryptography), the NINETEENTH composition: a per-record subkey, a hardened child of the identity node.
//! Where crypto/kumara_identity.rye derives the ONE identity a keeper signs with, this rung derives a DISTINCT signing key per record beneath
//! it — the hardened child m/44'/coin'/account'/index'/record' of the identity node — so a keeper does not reuse a single signing key across
//! every record they seal, while every subkey still descends deterministically from the one identity. It is the reason crypto/kumara_identity.rye
//! carries a chain_code at the identity node ("so a caller can derive further children (a per-record subkey) without re-walking from the seed"):
//! this rung reads that chain code and steps one level deeper.
//!
//! It authors NO new cryptography. The step from a parent node to a hardened child is crypto/slip10_ed25519.rye's own ckd_priv, already GREEN
//! against SLIP-0010's published ed25519 vectors; this module contributes only the wiring — reconstruct the parent node from an Identity (its
//! secret seed is the node key, its chain code the node chain code, its depth the Kumara path's fixed four), derive the hardened record child,
//! and read off the subkey's public key the same two independent ways crypto/kumara_identity.rye does: crypto/slip10_ed25519.rye's public form
//! (0x00 tag stripped) and crypto/ed25519_sign.rye's derive_public, asserted equal. Sealing a record AS a subkey adds no frame either — seal_as
//! writes a Grain Signed Carry v1 frame (crypto/signed_carry.rye) under the subkey's secret seed, and open_as reuses crypto/kumara_carry.rye's
//! binding to refuse any frame whose verified signer is not the subkey the reader named.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography · Rye-native, Monocypher-parity, audit-ready).
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md — Season G, "the library that serves Kumara
//! identity, Vault, Comlink, and the Lotus signed carry," which names "a per-record subkey carry (a child of the identity node)" as the identity
//! arc's next rung. The 1,024-round itinerary, Season 2 Journey 6 (Provenance): "a real document carries a Kumara-signed, content-addressed chain
//! of custody end to end." A per-record subkey is how one identity signs many records without spending the same key on each.
//! Kin: crypto/kumara_identity.rye (the identity this steps beneath, GREEN) · crypto/slip10_ed25519.rye (the ckd_priv this walks one level, GREEN) ·
//! crypto/ed25519_sign.rye + crypto/ed25519_verify.rye (the signature, GREEN) · crypto/signed_carry.rye + crypto/kumara_carry.rye (the frame this
//! seals and the binding it reuses, GREEN). Clean-room: SLIP-0010 and the path convention are public standards studied through their specs
//! (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md); no line is copied.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL, no network, no funds, no real device. Every identity and subkey here is a TEST key from a published
//! test-vector mnemonic; this module calls no std.crypto. Deriving a REAL keeper's subkeys and signing a real record with one stays the custody
//! gate (#3/#4) — this library derives and verifies a TEST identity's subkeys; it never holds the maintainer's key. Constant-time timing-safety of
//! the derivation and signing it composes stays crypto/CONSTANT_TIME.md's named horizon, unchanged here.
//!
//! ONE HONEST BOUNDARY, named as the next rung. ed25519/SLIP-0010 admits only hardened derivation — a subkey's public key CANNOT be derived from
//! the parent's public key alone, so a verifier who holds only the identity's public key cannot on their own prove a subkey descends from it. The
//! attestation that binds a subkey to its parent (the identity SIGNING a short "this subkey is mine at record r" statement, which a verifier then
//! checks against the identity's public key) is the named next rung — a signed delegation over exactly the door crypto/kumara_carry.rye already
//! opens. This rung proves what stands beneath that: the derivation is deterministic and well-separated per record, each subkey is a real working
//! signing key, and each subkey seals and opens AS ITSELF with the wrong-key binding refused.
//!
//! Parity is proven at selftest three replayable ways, no fabricated constant: (1) anchored to the identity arc — the subkey at record r equals
//! walking crypto/slip10_ed25519.rye's ckd_priv from the SAME identity node by hand, and its public key equals both slip10's public form (tag
//! stripped) and ed25519_sign's independent derive_public, all agreeing (the ckd_priv step itself proven one rung down against SLIP-0010's own
//! ed25519 vectors); (2) separation — the same identity + record reaches an identical subkey, a different record reaches a different subkey, and
//! every subkey differs from its parent identity; (3) the subkey is a REAL working signing key — it signs a message verify accepts, a flipped
//! message byte and a flipped signature byte are refused, and it seals a record that open_as verifies as the subkey while refusing the parent
//! identity and a sibling subkey with UnexpectedSigner.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run crypto/kumara_subkey.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const identity = @import("kumara_identity.rye");
const slip10 = @import("slip10_ed25519.rye");
const bip32 = @import("bip32.rye");
const bip39 = @import("bip39_seed.rye");
const kumara_path = @import("kumara_path.rye");
const signer = @import("ed25519_sign.rye");
const verifier = @import("ed25519_verify.rye");
const carry = @import("signed_carry.rye");
const kumara_carry = @import("kumara_carry.rye");

pub const kumara_subkey_version = "20260816.100250";

/// The 32-byte ed25519 secret seed a subkey signs with — the same width as the identity's own secret seed (both are ed25519 signing seeds).
pub const secret_len: u32 = identity.secret_len; // 32

/// The raw 32-byte ed25519 public key that names a subkey.
pub const public_len: u32 = identity.public_len; // 32

/// The chain code carried at the subkey node, so a caller could step further (a sub-subkey) without re-walking — the same forward door the
/// identity node offers, one level deeper.
pub const chain_len: u32 = identity.chain_len; // 32

/// The fixed depth of a Kumara identity node below the master: exactly the four hardened levels m/44'/coin'/account'/index' that
/// crypto/kumara_path.rye walks. A subkey is that node's hardened child, at depth five. Named rather than left as a bare 4 so the reconstruction
/// below states WHY the depth is what it is.
pub const identity_depth: u32 = 4;

/// A Kumara carry's payload bounds, re-exported through this door so a caller sealing AS a subkey sizes buffers by one name.
pub const max_payload_bytes: u32 = kumara_carry.max_payload_bytes;
pub const max_frame_bytes: u32 = kumara_carry.max_frame_bytes;

pub const KumaraSubkeyError = kumara_carry.KumaraCarryError;

/// The whole phrase→identity→subkey arc's error union: folding the mnemonic to an identity may raise a BIP-39 / path / SLIP-0010 error, and the
/// extra hardened step may raise a SLIP-0010 error. Named once so subkey_from_mnemonic states its contract honestly.
pub const KumaraSubkeyDeriveError =
    bip39.Bip39Error || kumara_path.KumaraPathError || slip10.Slip10Error;

/// One derived per-record subkey: the secret signing seed, the public key that names it, the chain code at its node, and the record index that
/// reached it beneath its parent identity. secret_seed is a secret; public_key, chain_code, and record are public.
pub const Subkey = struct {
    secret_seed: [secret_len]u8,
    public_key: [public_len]u8,
    chain_code: [chain_len]u8,
    record: u32,
};

/// Strip the SLIP-0010 33-byte public form (0x00 ‖ 32-byte key) to the raw 32-byte ed25519 public key a subkey is named by — the same strip
/// crypto/kumara_identity.rye applies to the identity node, asserting the domain tag it removes is the 0x00 SLIP-0010 expects.
fn strip_domain_tag(slip_public: *const [slip10.public_len]u8) [public_len]u8 {
    // invariant: the SLIP-0010 public form always opens with the 0x00 ed25519 domain tag (crypto/slip10_ed25519.rye public_key).
    assert(slip_public[0] == 0x00);
    var out: [public_len]u8 = undefined;
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < public_len) : (k += 1) {
        // invariant: the 32-byte key sits after exactly one tag byte, so index k reads slip_public[1 + k] within the 33-byte form.
        assert((1 + k) < slip10.public_len);
        out[k] = slip_public[1 + k];
    }
    return out;
}

/// Reconstruct the parent identity node from an Identity, so ckd_priv can step one level deeper without re-walking from the seed. The Identity
/// carries exactly the node's key (its secret seed), chain code, and — by the Kumara path convention — depth four; this rung assembles those into
/// the slip10.Node ckd_priv expects.
fn parent_node(id: *const identity.Identity) slip10.Node {
    // invariant: an Identity's three node-bearing fields are exactly the widths a slip10.Node holds — the reconstruction loses nothing.
    assert(identity.secret_len == slip10.key_len and identity.chain_len == slip10.chain_code_len);
    return slip10.Node{
        .key = id.secret_seed,
        .chain_code = id.chain_code,
        .depth = identity_depth,
    };
}

/// Derive the per-record subkey beneath an identity: the hardened child of the identity node at `record`. Reads the identity's secret seed and
/// chain code; the subkey it returns holds its own secret seed (a secret the caller keeps) and public key (public). TEST identities only — a real
/// identity's subkey reaches the custody gate.
pub fn derive_subkey(id: *const identity.Identity, record: u32) slip10.Slip10Error!Subkey {
    // invariant: the record level stays below 2³¹ before the hardening bit is added — a value at or above it would collide with the offset.
    if (record >= bip32.hardened_offset) return error.NotHardened;

    const parent = parent_node(id);
    // invariant: the parent sits at the Kumara identity depth, so its hardened child sits at depth five, well under slip10's ceiling.
    assert(parent.depth == identity_depth);
    const child = try slip10.ckd_priv(&parent, record + bip32.hardened_offset);
    // invariant: a derived child sits exactly one level below its parent, and the depth is what
    // a serialized key publishes -- a wrong one names a different position in the tree
    assert(child.depth == identity_depth + 1);

    const slip_public = slip10.public_key(&child);
    const public_key = strip_domain_tag(&slip_public);
    const direct = signer.derive_public(&child.key);
    // invariant: the two independent public derivations agree byte-for-byte — the subkey's public key is unambiguous, exactly as the identity's is.
    var k: u32 = 0;
    // invariant: the derived key is compared byte for byte against the one computed directly,
    // so the two paths are proven to agree rather than assumed to
    while (k < public_len) : (k += 1) assert(public_key[k] == direct[k]);

    return Subkey{
        .secret_seed = child.key,
        .public_key = public_key,
        .chain_code = child.chain_code,
        .record = record,
    };
}

/// The whole arc in one call: fold a mnemonic to a Kumara identity, then derive its per-record subkey. TEST mnemonics only — the maintainer's own
/// phrase reaches the custody gate.
pub fn subkey_from_mnemonic(
    mnemonic: []const u8,
    passphrase: []const u8,
    coin_type: u32,
    account: u32,
    index: u32,
    record: u32,
) KumaraSubkeyDeriveError!Subkey {
    const id = try identity.from_mnemonic(mnemonic, passphrase, coin_type, account, index);
    return derive_subkey(&id, record);
}

/// Sign a message with a subkey's secret seed — the thin wrapper that lets a caller make a per-record signature through this door. Reads the
/// secret seed; the signature it returns is public.
pub fn sign(subkey: *const Subkey, msg: []const u8) signer.SignError![64]u8 {
    return signer.sign(&subkey.secret_seed, msg);
}

/// Verify a signature against a subkey's public key — the public counterpart of sign, so a verifier need hold only the 32-byte public key.
pub fn verify(public_key: *const [public_len]u8, msg: []const u8, sig: *const [64]u8) verifier.VerifyError!void {
    return verifier.verify(sig, msg, public_key);
}

/// Seal an opaque payload AS a subkey, writing a Grain Signed Carry v1 frame into `out` and returning its length. The frame's signer field is the
/// subkey's own public key by construction — signed_carry derives the signer from the same secret seed the subkey holds.
pub fn seal_as(subkey: *const Subkey, payload: []const u8, out: []u8) carry.SignedCarryError!u32 {
    // invariant: a subkey's secret seed is the exact 32-byte width signed_carry seals under (both are the ed25519 signing seed).
    assert(secret_len == carry.signer_len);
    const frame_len = try carry.seal(&subkey.secret_seed, payload, out);

    // invariant: the frame's embedded signer is exactly this subkey's public key — the carry and subkey rungs agree by construction.
    const embedded_signer = out[carry.magic_len..][0..carry.signer_len];
    var k: u32 = 0;
    // invariant: the derived key is compared byte for byte against the one computed directly,
    // so the two paths are proven to agree rather than assumed to
    while (k < public_len) : (k += 1) assert(embedded_signer[k] == subkey.public_key[k]);
    return frame_len;
}

/// Open a subkey carry and BIND it to a named subkey: reuse crypto/kumara_carry.rye's open_as, which runs signed_carry's verify-before-trust and
/// then refuses, with UnexpectedSigner, any frame whose verified signer is not the subkey the reader named. Reads only public bytes; holds no key.
pub fn open_as(frame: []const u8, expected_public: *const [public_len]u8) KumaraSubkeyError!carry.Opened {
    return kumara_carry.open_as(frame, expected_public);
}

// ---- selftest ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Fill a buffer with a deterministic spread of test bytes — the sibling compositions' generator shape, seeded so each value differs and the first
/// byte is nonzero. Used for TEST payloads only.
fn fill_test(buf: []u8, seed: u8) void {
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < buf.len) : (i += 1) buf[i] = @as(u8, @truncate(@as(u32, i) *% (@as(u32, seed) | 1) +% @as(u32, seed) +% 7));
    if (buf.len > 0) buf[0] |= 1;
}

fn bytes_eq(a: []const u8, b: []const u8) bool {
    if (a.len != b.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < a.len) : (i += 1) if (a[i] != b[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

// BIP-39's own published Trezor vector 1 — a REAL published phrase, so the identity a subkey descends from stands on a standard's own answer.
const trezor_mnemonic = "abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon about";
const trezor_passphrase = "TREZOR";

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    const test_coin: u32 = 7; // a registered Grain SLIP-44 coin type is a named horizon; the tests pass an explicit slot.

    var frame_buf: [max_frame_bytes]u8 = undefined;

    // The keeper's identity — a TEST identity from the published Trezor mnemonic. NOT a real Kumara identity.
    const id = try identity.from_mnemonic(trezor_mnemonic, trezor_passphrase, test_coin, 0, 0);

    // 1. ANCHORED TO THE IDENTITY ARC. The subkey at record 0 equals walking slip10's ckd_priv from the SAME identity node by hand, and its public
    //    key equals both slip10's public form (0x00 tag stripped) and ed25519_sign's independent derive_public — the same 32 bytes three ways.
    const sub0 = try derive_subkey(&id, 0);
    {
        const parent = slip10.Node{ .key = id.secret_seed, .chain_code = id.chain_code, .depth = identity_depth };
        const by_hand = try slip10.ckd_priv(&parent, 0 + bip32.hardened_offset);
        if (!bytes_eq(sub0.secret_seed[0..], by_hand.key[0..])) return error.SubkeySecretDrift;
        if (!bytes_eq(sub0.chain_code[0..], by_hand.chain_code[0..])) return error.SubkeyChainDrift;

        const slip_public = slip10.public_key(&by_hand);
        if (slip_public[0] != 0x00) return error.SubkeyDomainTagWrong;
        if (!bytes_eq(sub0.public_key[0..], slip_public[1..])) return error.SubkeyPublicVsSlipMismatch;
        const direct = signer.derive_public(&by_hand.key);
        if (!bytes_eq(sub0.public_key[0..], direct[0..])) return error.SubkeyPublicVsDirectMismatch;
    }

    // 2. SEPARATION. The same identity + record reaches an identical subkey; a different record reaches a different subkey; and every subkey differs
    //    from its parent identity — one identity, many distinct per-record keys.
    {
        const again = try derive_subkey(&id, 0);
        if (!bytes_eq(sub0.public_key[0..], again.public_key[0..])) return error.SubkeyNotDeterministic;
        if (!bytes_eq(sub0.secret_seed[0..], again.secret_seed[0..])) return error.SubkeySecretNotDeterministic;

        const records = [_]u32{ 1, 2, 5, 1000, 2147483647 };
        for (records) |r| {
            const sr = try derive_subkey(&id, r);
            if (bytes_eq(sub0.public_key[0..], sr.public_key[0..])) return error.RecordsNotSeparated;
            if (bytes_eq(id.public_key[0..], sr.public_key[0..])) return error.SubkeyEqualsParent;
        }
        // record 0 itself is not the parent identity — a child node is a distinct key from its parent.
        if (bytes_eq(sub0.public_key[0..], id.public_key[0..])) return error.Sub0EqualsParent;
    }

    // 3. THE ONE-CALL ARC agrees with the two steps by hand — subkey_from_mnemonic folds phrase → identity → subkey without drift.
    {
        const by_call = try subkey_from_mnemonic(trezor_mnemonic, trezor_passphrase, test_coin, 0, 0, 0);
        if (!bytes_eq(by_call.public_key[0..], sub0.public_key[0..])) return error.OneCallPublicDrift;
        if (!bytes_eq(by_call.secret_seed[0..], sub0.secret_seed[0..])) return error.OneCallSecretDrift;
    }

    // 4. THE SUBKEY IS A REAL WORKING SIGNING KEY. It signs a message verify accepts; a flipped message byte and a flipped signature byte each
    //    refuse — the whole point of a per-record key, proven.
    {
        const message = "Grain — one identity, a fresh key for each record it keeps.";
        const sig = try sign(&sub0, message);
        try verify(&sub0.public_key, message, &sig);

        var tampered: [message.len]u8 = undefined;
        var k: u32 = 0;
        while (k < message.len) : (k += 1) tampered[k] = message[k];
        tampered[7] ^= 0x01;
        if (verify(&sub0.public_key, tampered[0..], &sig)) |_| return error.SubTamperMsgAccepted else |_| {}

        var bad_sig = sig;
        bad_sig[12] ^= 0x80;
        if (verify(&sub0.public_key, message, &bad_sig)) |_| return error.SubTamperSigAccepted else |_| {}
    }

    // 5. SEAL AND OPEN AS THE SUBKEY across a spread of payload lengths — open_as returns the payload only when bound to the subkey's own public
    //    key, and refuses both the PARENT identity and a SIBLING subkey with UnexpectedSigner (a valid signature from the wrong key, caught).
    {
        const sibling = try derive_subkey(&id, 1);
        const lengths = [_]u32{ 0, 1, 44, 200, 4096, max_payload_bytes };
        for (lengths) |plen| {
            var payload_buf: [max_payload_bytes]u8 = undefined;
            const payload = payload_buf[0..plen];
            fill_test(payload, @truncate(plen +% 3));
            const flen = try seal_as(&sub0, payload, frame_buf[0..]);

            const opened = try open_as(frame_buf[0..flen], &sub0.public_key);
            if (!bytes_eq(opened.signer[0..], sub0.public_key[0..])) return error.OpenedSignerWrong;
            if (!bytes_eq(opened.payload, payload)) return error.OpenedPayloadWrong;

            // The parent identity's public key refuses this subkey-sealed record — the record is the subkey's, not the identity's.
            if (open_as(frame_buf[0..flen], &id.public_key)) |_| return error.ParentAccepted else |e| {
                if (e != error.UnexpectedSigner) return error.ParentWrongError;
            }
            // A sibling subkey refuses it too — per-record keys do not open each other's records.
            if (open_as(frame_buf[0..flen], &sibling.public_key)) |_| return error.SiblingAccepted else |e| {
                if (e != error.UnexpectedSigner) return error.SiblingWrongError;
            }
        }
    }

    // 6. EDGE. A record at or above the hardened offset (2³¹) collides with the hardening bit and is refused NotHardened.
    {
        const at_offset: u32 = 1 << 31;
        if (derive_subkey(&id, at_offset)) |_| return error.OffsetRecordAccepted else |e| {
            if (e != error.NotHardened) return error.WrongOffsetRecord;
        }
    }

    print("GREEN crypto-kumara-subkey: the NINETEENTH composition authored in pure Rye — a per-record subkey, the hardened child " ++
        "m/44'/coin'/account'/index'/record' of the identity node, so a keeper signs each record with a DISTINCT key while every subkey descends " ++
        "deterministically from the one Kumara identity (the reason crypto/kumara_identity carries a chain code at its node). Authors NO new " ++
        "cryptography and adds NO new frame — the step from identity node to hardened child is crypto/slip10_ed25519's own ckd_priv, already GREEN " ++
        "against SLIP-0010's ed25519 vectors; sealing AS a subkey writes a Grain Signed Carry v1 frame under the subkey's seed and reuses " ++
        "crypto/kumara_carry's binding. Proven GREEN on metal three replayable ways with no fabricated constant: (1) anchored to the identity arc — " ++
        "the subkey at record r equals walking ckd_priv from the same identity node by hand, its public key equal to both slip10's public form " ++
        "(0x00 tag stripped) and ed25519_sign's independent derive_public; (2) separation — the same identity + record reaches an identical subkey, " ++
        "a different record a different subkey, and every subkey differs from its parent identity; (3) the subkey is a REAL working signing key — it " ++
        "signs a message verify accepts, a flipped message byte and a flipped signature byte refuse, and it seals a record open_as verifies as the " ++
        "subkey while refusing the parent identity and a sibling subkey with UnexpectedSigner, across six payload lengths (empty through 64 KiB); a " ++
        "record at the hardened offset refuses NotHardened. Purely local — TEST identities from published vectors, no real identity key, no network, " ++
        "no funds, no real device; deriving a REAL keeper's subkeys and signing a real record with one stays the custody gate, and constant-time " ++
        "timing-safety stays the Season's named horizon. The parent→subkey attestation — the identity SIGNING that a subkey is its own — is the " ++
        "named next rung, a signed delegation over exactly this door; this rung proves the derivation and per-record separation beneath it.\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
